# The Semgrep job's tool.
#
# pip-compile turns this into semgrep.txt: every package, exact versions AND hashes, installed with
# `--require-hashes`. Dependabot keeps it current by re-running pip-compile on the Python its
# header names (3.11), so a bump never pins a set that cannot be installed together. Edit
# THIS file, then regenerate (run in python:3.11-slim):
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --strip-extras --unsafe-package=mcp \
#     --unsafe-package=opentelemetry-api --unsafe-package=opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-common \
#     --unsafe-package=opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http \
#     --unsafe-package=opentelemetry-instrumentation \
#     --unsafe-package=opentelemetry-instrumentation-requests \
#     --unsafe-package=opentelemetry-instrumentation-threading --unsafe-package=opentelemetry-proto \
#     --unsafe-package=opentelemetry-sdk --unsafe-package=opentelemetry-semantic-conventions \
#     --unsafe-package=opentelemetry-util-http --output-file=semgrep.txt semgrep.in
# then semgrep-mcp.txt from semgrep-mcp.in and semgrep-otel.txt from semgrep-otel.in (each header
# says how).
#
# mcp is left out of semgrep.txt (--unsafe-package) and pinned on its own in semgrep-mcp.txt.
# semgrep 1.183.0, the release semgrep.txt pins (1.179.0 to 1.182.x alike), declares mcp==1.29.0
# exactly, and 1.29.0 has GHSA-84m7-p3x7-pcfv (StreamableHTTP sessions without a bound; fixed in
# 1.30.0), so no set that honours semgrep's pin is safe. mcp 1.30.0 declares the same dependencies
# as 1.29.0, all of them in semgrep.txt; semgrep-mcp.txt repeats them at semgrep.txt's versions
# (its -c line holds them there), so the two files together are the whole closure. The job
# installs both with --no-deps, hash-checked, and then runs pip check, which may report only
# semgrep's own mcp pin against the version semgrep-mcp.txt installs; a package either file lacks
# still fails the job, by name.
# semgrep scan runs on mcp 1.30.0: it imports mcp at startup for its MCP server, which a scan never
# serves. Dependabot keeps --unsafe-package (it reads it back from the header) and updates
# semgrep-mcp.txt on its own.
#
# The OpenTelemetry family is left out the same way and pinned in semgrep-otel.txt. semgrep 1.183.0
# (1.185.0, the newest, alike) declares opentelemetry-api, -sdk and -exporter-otlp-proto-http
# ~=1.37.0, and every 1.37 api has GHSA-mf5v-w3w3-f6cg (quadratic traceparent parsing; fixed in
# 1.45.2) and GHSA-xcq7-p3pf-p88h (unbounded tracestate parsing; fixed in 1.45.1). The sdk requires
# one exact api, the exporter one sdk release line, and the betas (instrumentation, semantic
# conventions) one exact release of each other and of the api, so the family moves together: every
# opentelemetry-* package semgrep pulls in is an --unsafe-package here, and semgrep-otel.txt pins
# the matching set (1.45.2 with the 0.66b2 betas), its other dependencies held at semgrep.txt's
# versions. pip check may report semgrep's three ~=1.37.0 pins against what that file installs
# (semgrep-otel.in records them); semgrep's ~=0.58b0 instrumentation pins are met by 0.66b2.
# semgrep scan imports the family at startup for its tracing (--trace, off by default; the job does
# not pass it). The scan was run on both sets over this repository: identical findings.
#
# UNDO when a semgrep release allows mcp>=1.30.0: drop --unsafe-package=mcp and semgrep-mcp.in/.txt.
# UNDO when a semgrep release allows opentelemetry-api>=1.45.2 (and the sdk and exporter with it):
# drop the opentelemetry --unsafe-package options and semgrep-otel.in/.txt. With both gone, install
# semgrep.txt alone without --no-deps, and remove the job's pip-check step.
#
# The same split was made once before, for PyJWT (semgrep 1.178.0 pinned a vulnerable ~=2.13.0),
# and undone when semgrep 1.179.0 allowed pyjwt>=2.15.0.
semgrep
